\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'a8\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls2\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a8}}\ls2\adjustright {\i\fs22\lang1033 Greetings}{\lang1033 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang1033 
\par \page }{\b\fs24\ul\lang1033 Introduction
\par }{\lang1033 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\i\lang1033 "Weave a circle round him thrice,
\par and close your eyes with holy dread,
\par for he on honey dew hath fed,
\par and drank the milk of paradise."
\par - Samuel Taylor Colleridge}{\lang1033 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang1033 
\par DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[ is the latest in my series of DiViNE INTERVENTiON war programs, and, at least for quite a while, probably the last.
\par 
\par DiViNE ]I[ contains, in one single powerful packages, all the weapons that I could manage to implement under Windows' Winsock 2.0 implementation. Although it may seem like a powerful package, there are many weapons that could not be included because of Winsock's restrictions, when compared to Linux's BSD socket implementations.
\par 
\par The DiViNE INTERVENTiON series represents, however, a definite step forward in the quality and design of underground programs. Previously, programs such as these were not always well written, were often bulky, badly designed, often with no documentation, and just didn't look particularly good. With the DiViNE series, I have attempted to merge not only the raw power of knowledge, but a good and friendly design, a reasonable size, and proper documentation. I hope, and }{\i\lang1033 expect}{\lang1033 , that from this point on underground coderz will produce programs of a significantly higher quality. It is the nature of software to improve markedly from version to version. My challenge to other underground coders is simply this: }{\b\i\lang1033 compete, or fade away}{\lang1033 .
\par 
\par As for DiViNE ]I[ being the last, this is for dual reasons. Firstly, I can't think of much else that I could add to DiViNE using Winsock. Secondly, this year I'm starting web authoring with GRySTAL, and am starting work on an HTML editor. These projects take preference because they are commercial, whereas underground programming is merely a time-consuming hobby.
\par 
\par I would also like to comment on the apparently sadly lazy attitudes of many underground web masters. I spend a lot of time surfing around, and mailing these web authors, in an attempt to get them to include my programs, or link me. Some never reply, and of those that do, many take an extremely long time to even look at my programs, }{\i\lang1033 if}{\lang1033  they do}{\i\lang1033 . It is in the interests of a web site to stay up to date and current on the most powerful programs out}{\lang1033  - and therefore in their interests to at least evaluate my software quickly. I've authored four web sites for free, and of these, I maintain three of them, keeping them up to date and making sure they're working perfectly. I do this without getting paid - those with advertising on their pages have significantly less excuse.
\par 
\par Please refrain from using DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[ indiscriminately - especially the meaner portions of it. This program was never intended for use in terrorizing the general public, and it is an absolute certainty that frequent abuse of the weapons in it will get you into trouble with your ISP.
\par 
\par The source code for DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[ is }{\b\lang1033 not}{\lang1033  freely available, as was the case with DiViNE ][.  This is because the weapons contained within it should not become commonly available - they are available (I got them, didn't I?) but it takes a lot of work, and energy, and understanding, which is precisely what it should take. However, to corporations and companies seeking to protect themselves and their clients against DiViNE ]I[, the source code will be available for a cash payment of US $1000 - however, for smaller companies, this is negotiable. There are holes in the system, and they need to be fixed.
\par 
\par DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[, whether it represents (as I believe) a significant milestone in the development and sophistication of war programs or not, is definitely one of the, if not }{\i\lang1033 the}{\lang1033 , most powerful programs of it's kind available on the planet. It therefore deserves respect - respect in the sense that you do not abuse it and the power and defiance that it provides, and respect in that you do not mess with it in any way.
\par 
\par  - Pri$m.   (February 1998)
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\ul\lang1033 Official Distribution Sites
\par }{\lang1033 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\i\lang1033 "You don't need eyes to see, you need vision." - Faithless
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang1033 
\par The following sites are official distribution sites for both the DiViNE series of war programs and all of Pri$m's underground software:
\par 
\par DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[ (ZA Mirror)            : http://home.pix.za/gk/prism/sites/divint
\par DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[ (GeoCities Mirror) : http://www.geocities.com/SiliconValley/Way/2618
\par DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[ (DarkSide Mirror)  : http:// 38.241.168.231/DiViNT
\par 
\par Pri$m's Domain                                                       : http://home.pix.za/gk/prism
\par Midnight BBS                                                          : http://www.websiteplus.com/midnight
\par 
\par Pri$m's Domain is Pri$m's personal web site. Should you wish to contact him, the page will have both his email address and his ICQ UIN.  The page contains all of his FileZ, Scrawlingz, a guided tour of The Cave (otherwise known as his room), a Bio Profile, and a collection of Linx to the far corners of the 'Net. And of course you'd BETTER sign the guest book!
\par 
\par The Midnight BBS - A great page, H/P/A/C/V/Warez/Mail Bombers, Message
\par Board, Midi & Lyric of the Week, Chat, Java Games, Anti-Virus, FTP,
\par and More!  Drop by, it's in both English & French.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\ul\lang1033 Using DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[
\par }{\lang1033 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\i\lang1033 "Do, or do not. There is no try." - Yoda
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang1033 
\par DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[ has been split into sections, where each section groups together weapons with a common function. Please see the heading specific for that section for information on using the weapons contained within it.
\par 
\par DiViNE INTERVENTiON has been coded and designed to be pleasant and easy to use, as well as aesthetically pleasing. If you find it difficult to use or understand any part of this program, you should think long and hard about whether you }{\i\lang1033 should}{\lang1033  be using it.
\par 
\par Should you not understand what a particular part of the program does, hover your mouse cursor over it - be it an edit box, or a button - and a hint will appear that should to some extent explain what that particular component's function is.
\par 
\par Please note that to either hide or restore DiViNE's main window, you can either double click on the Pri$m icon in the system tray, or you can right click on that icon and choose either 'Restore' or 'Hide'. Also, if your copy is running, and you switch to another window to do something, (eg get an IP), then you can bring the DiViNE window back to the front either using ALT-TAB, or by left clicking once on the icon in the system tray
\par }{\b\fs24\ul\lang1033 
\par Obtaining an IP Address
\par }{\lang1033 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\i\lang1033 "Therefore it can be said that victorious warriors win first, and then go to battle, while defeated warriors go to battle first, and then seek to win." - Sun Tzu, the Art of War
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang1033 
\par Since most of the weapons in DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[ require a target IP address, this section will attempt to explain what an IP address is and how to get one.
\par 
\par An IP address is a unique number that identifies a machine on the Internet. This number can be used to attempt to connect to that machine. An IP address is usually entered in }{\i\lang1033 dot notation}{\lang1033  - for example, 196.25.11.14. All machines on the same subnet should only differ in the last number.
\par 
\par The most common places to obtain an IP address are IRC and ICQ. On IRC, simply type "/dns <put their nick here>", and on ICQ, in the User's Info section there is a Last IP Address field.
\par 
\par Sometimes, email headers may contain an IP address, but the chances are that the user has since disconnected. }{\i\lang1033 Please note that a user's IP address does NOT remain the same when they disconnect and re-connect}{\lang1033 .
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\ul\lang1033 Components
\par }{\lang1033 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\i\lang1033 "Everything should be made as simple as possible, but not simpler." - Albert Einstein
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang1033 
\par Your copy of DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[ should contain:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs20\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls4\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls4\adjustright {\lang1033 Divint3.exe (Program)
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs20\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls4\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls4\adjustright {\lang1033 Divint.doc (User's Manual - this file)
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs20\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls4\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls4\adjustright {\lang1033 Divhist.doc (The History of DiViNE INTERVENTiON)
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs20\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls4\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls4\adjustright {\lang1033 Mlist.txt (Parsed text file containing mailing list names and addresses)
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs20\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \fi-360\li360\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls4\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls4\adjustright {\lang1033 File_Id.diz (File ID information)
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang1033 
\par }{\b\fs24\ul\lang1033 RETRiBUTiON
\par }{\lang1033 
\par RETRiBUTiON is DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I['s nuking section. It includes two weapons used to nuke a user's machine or connection - FyRE and BRiMSTONE. Note that an IRC nuker is also included in INCANTATIONZ.
\par 
\par }{\i\ul\lang1033 FyRE
\par }{\lang1033 
\par FyRE is an OOB nuker. It exploits a bug in Windows 95 and NT that causes them to throw a system exception when sent OOB (}{\i\lang1033 Out Of Band}{\lang1033 ) data via their NetBios ports (139 for Win95, 135 for WinNT). Note that there is a patch for this.
\par 
\par A nuke can do anything from merely show a blue screen, to killing connections, to forcing a reboot - the effects are determined by the exact situation that the target machine is in when it is nuked. You can use a port watcher - the most popular is Skream's - to notify you when someone attempts to nuke you. However, it is a far better idea to obtain the nuke patch - now available from the DiViNE web sites (in the FAQ) which invalidates a port watcher.
\par 
\par OOB nuking has become so common that many users now have the patch - and it has become a significantly less potent weapon than it was when DiViNE ][ was released.
\par 
\par }{\i\ul\lang1033 BRiMSTONE
\par }{\lang1033 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\i\lang1033 BRiMSTONE is based almost entirely upon RhadWare's Click v2.2, and it's Visual C++ source code. It is, in essence, a slightly simplified version of Click.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang1033 
\par BRiMSTONE is an IRC Nuker. It allows you to nuke a person }{\i\lang1033 off}{\lang1033  IRC - disconnecting him.
\par It can also be used as a connection nuker - it should, in theory, be able to kill }{\i\lang1033 any}{\lang1033  connection, with the right ports and server.
\par 
\par To use BRiMSTONE, enter the victim's IP address in the Client edit box, and the server address (their name address will be fine - typically irc.something.net) in the Server edit box. Adjust the ports as desired - note that some IRC servers only allow usage of port 6667. (for this, set the server ports to }{\i\lang1033 6667 to 6667}{\lang1033 )
\par 
\par You can also adjust the speed and number of packets to be sent based on the speed of your connection and the speed of the server. Experiment for best results. Click "Start" to begin the nuking.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\ul\lang1033 PLAGUEz
\par }{\lang1033 
\par PLAGUEz is DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I['s flooding section. It contains tools that attempt to disable or slow down a connection by }{\i\lang1033 flooding}{\lang1033  it with data.
\par 
\par }{\i\ul\lang1033 FLOODz
\par }{\lang1033 \line FLOODz is a}{ ping flooder. When supplied with the target IP address, and a packet size, it will proceed to flood the user\rquote s IP with successive ping requests, thereby consuming a massive amount of bandwidth and slowing his access down. This is because for each ping request, the target machine must receive all of the data in the ping, and then send a }{\i 'pong'}{.
\par 
\par The pinging should be entirely invisible to the user, except perhaps for his modem receive- and send-data lights going off like crazy.
\par 
\par Ping flooding can be an exceptionally effective way to virtually disable a user\rquote s \lquote Net access for the duration of the flood. While DiViNE ]['s ping flooder was not very effective, the ping flooder in DiViNE ]I[ has reverted back to DiViNE I's method, and is thus very effective.
\par 
\par }{\i\ul 
\par LOCUSTS}{\i\ul\lang1033 
\par }{\lang1033 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\i\lang1033 LOCUSTS is based almost entirely upon the Linux source code for icqflood, by enkil^ and irQ
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang1033 
\par LOCUSTS is an ICQ flooder. It will flood the ICQ client of the machine with messages from random UINs. These UINs will all appear on the victim's Contact List under the 'Unknown' section.
\par 
\par The flooding is very fast on your side - but it will take the user ages to close all, say, 1000 messages, and then delete those UINs from his contact list.
\par 
\par There is also an option to attempt an ICQ 'kill' - this only works on ICQ clients older than ICQ '98. (this is most of them) The option will shut down the ICQ client on the target machine, if successful. There is no way of telling remotely how successful the operation was.
\par 
\par To use the ICQ flooder, enter that target's IP address, the port to start scanning from and stop scanning at (it is usually fine to just leave these as set by default), and the number of messages to send. To enable the 'Kill', just check the 'Attempt ICQ Kill' option.
\par 
\par LOCUSTS will first scan for the target's open port, and then send the messages or kill via that port.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\ul\lang1033 OMENz
\par }{\lang1033 
\par OMENz is DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I['s mail bombing section. It includes two weapons used to send massive amounts of electronic mail to a target user's account - HELLFyRE, and DEMONz.
\par 
\par }{\i\ul\lang1033 HELLFyRE
\par }{\lang1033 
\par HELLFyRE was named to reflect it's joint descent from DiViNE INTERVENTiON ][ and QuickFyre. It constitutes, however, is a drastic improvement over both of these programs. Sufficed to say, HELLFyRE has, under test conditions, sent 1000 mails in less than }{\i\lang1033 two minutes}{\lang1033  on a 28.8K modem.
\par 
\par HELLFyRE utilizes multiple servers, and multiple connections per server. For each connection (number of connections per server }{\i\lang1033 times}{\lang1033  number of servers), a thread with a separate socket is spawned, which sends off mails. Unlike some mail bombers, the threads are not given a specific number of mails to send each - they instead send independently until the target is reached - therefore allowing faster individual connections to send more mail, and minimizing the speed penalty of slower individual connections.
\par 
\par To use HELLFyRE, enter the target details, the number of copies and the number of connections per server. (The more the merrier; I recommend around 50 for large amounts of mail) Then, add servers from the combo box on the left to the servers list box on the right using the transfer right button - the button with the red arrow pointing right. (Use the button with the red arrow pointing left to remove the selected server from the list) Then enter a message, and hit send.
\par 
\par Note that HELLFyRE does not send truly anonymous emails; on an }{\i\lang1033 anonymous server}{\lang1033 , the recipient is not given any warning that the from address is bogus. However, some of these servers may record your IP address in the mail headers - although most mail programs don't show this anyway. The best plan is to send yourself just one mail, and have a look at it - see what kind of warning, if any, it gives.
\par 
\par Please be }{\b\lang1033 careful}{\lang1033  with this weapon. While it may take only two minutes to send 1000 mails, it takes a very long time for the target to download them - he's }{\b\lang1033 not}{\lang1033  going to be a happy man.
\par 
\par }{\i\ul\lang1033 DEMONz
\par }{\lang1033 
\par DEMONz is simpler to use than HELLFyRE, in that it requires less information. DEMONz fakes mails }{\i\lang1033 from}{\lang1033  the given target user, to about three thousand mailing lists, requesting a subscription. Many of these lists will automatically subscribe - some will send a confirmation of receipt message and then a request for authentication - however, the results of this are annoying on an epic scale.
\par 
\par The target will receive all mail sent to the lists that have accepted him for subscription. Worse, the target will have to individually unsubscribe to }{\i\lang1033 each and every mailing list}{\lang1033 . DEMONz is therefore probably the meanest weapon available in DiViNE ]I[. Be }{\b\lang1033 exceptionally}{\lang1033  careful and cautious with it.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\ul\lang1033 INCANTATiONz
\par }{\lang1033 
\par INCANTATiONz is DiViNE's IRC section. It includes two IRC 'bots'.
\par 
\par }{\i\ul\lang1033 HAiLSTORM
\par }{\lang1033 
\par HAiLSTORM is an IRC DCC flood bot. It allows you to specify the target nick, and IRC server to use, and the nick of the bot.
\par 
\par Each bot will send a DCC chat request to the victim. Most people have their IRC clients set to auto-accept - in which case their DCC chat will be flooded by the bot.  They may choose to ignore the request, in which case the bot will be not be able to flood - they will simply annoy the user with their DCC chat requests.
\par 
\par If the user accepts, depending on the relative speed of your two modems, his IRC client may lock up.
\par 
\par }{\i\ul\lang1033 WHyRLWiND
\par }{\lang1033 
\par WhyRLWiND is an IRC Flood Bot. It allows you to specify the target nick, and IRC server to use, and will launch the given number of bots, each of which will repeatedly flood the target user with private messages, saying whatever you enter in the Flood Text field repeatedly.
\par 
\par When the bots are disconnected (usually an excess flood kill) they will re-connect and continue flooding.
\par 
\par You can also specify the base bot nick - for example, if you specify 'killerjello', and four bots, you will have killerjello1, killerjello2, killerjello3 and killerjello4.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\ul\lang1033 The Coding of DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[
\par }{\lang1033 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\i\lang1033 "Some dance to remember, some dance to forget." - The Eagles
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang1033 
\par DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[ was coded in Borland C++Builder and Borland C++ v5.02
\par 
\par During the coding of DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[, Pri$m coded almost entirely in the early hours of the morning, during which he knew he would not be distracted. Therefore, DiViNE ]I[ was written almost entirely between 1 and 4 in the morning. Pri$m saw at least three sunrises, slept infrequently, ate at sporadic intervals and, yes, actually did go out between coding.
\par 
\par Almost all of DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[ was coded within Pri$m's Cave in Gauteng, South Africa. Portions of it were also created in Room 312, Protea Hotel, Umhlanga, in KwaZulu \\ Natal, South Africa, while Pri$m was there on holiday.
\par 
\par Not all of DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[ was coded by Pri$m from scratch - some interface elements were downloaded as FreeWare components from the Internet. Their authors, and the components used, are mentioned in the Acknowledgements section. BRiMSTONE, the IRC nuker, is heavily based on the Visual C++ source code released by RhadWare for Click v2.2. LOCUSTS, the ICQ flooder and nuke, is heavily based on the Linux source code for icqflood, by enkil^ and irQ.
\par 
\par At almost all times during the coding of DiViNE INTERVENTiON ]I[, Pri$m had music on in the background. This music consisted mostly of U2, the Counting Crows and Nirvana Unplugged.
\par 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\i\lang1033 Vast quantities of individual coco pops, lifesavers, coke cans, romany creams, tins of tuna, chocolate biscuits, peanut butter sandwiches, chicken foo yong and sparkles gave their lives selflessly for the creation of this program. Their memory will not be forgotten}{\i\fs22\lang1033 .
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright {\lang1033 
